1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,943
It's a Saturday morning in nineteen fifty-seven,

2
00:00:02,943 --> 00:00:05,150
and you're standing at the corner of

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:05,886
Main Street.

4
00:00:06,066 --> 00:00:08,524
The sun is shining on the brick

5
00:00:08,524 --> 00:00:09,317
buildings.

6
00:00:09,497 --> 00:00:12,151
Cars are parked at an angle along

7
00:00:12,151 --> 00:00:12,875
the curb.

8
00:00:13,055 --> 00:00:16,085
A bell rings over a shop door.

9
00:00:16,265 --> 00:00:18,801
Somebody waves at you from across the

10
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:20,788
street, because in this town,

11
00:00:20,788 --> 00:00:22,569
everybody knows everybody.

12
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:24,762
In nineteen fifty-seven,

13
00:00:24,762 --> 00:00:27,373
almost every town in America had a

14
00:00:27,373 --> 00:00:28,678
street like this.

15
00:00:28,858 --> 00:00:31,873
It might have been called Main Street,

16
00:00:31,873 --> 00:00:35,046
Front Street, Broadway or Market Street.

17
00:00:35,226 --> 00:00:36,682
But it was always the heart of

18
00:00:36,682 --> 00:00:37,119
the town.

19
00:00:37,299 --> 00:00:39,013
It was where you shopped,

20
00:00:39,013 --> 00:00:41,139
ate, banked, got your hair cut,

21
00:00:41,139 --> 00:00:43,060
saw a movie and caught up on

22
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:43,951
all the news.

23
00:00:44,301 --> 00:00:46,298
So let's take a walk.

24
00:00:46,478 --> 00:00:47,604
Door by door,

25
00:00:47,604 --> 00:00:48,903
block by block,

26
00:00:48,903 --> 00:00:52,195
down a typical American Main Street in

27
00:00:52,195 --> 00:00:54,013
nineteen fifty-seven.

28
00:00:54,193 --> 00:00:56,020
If you grew up in a small

29
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:59,161
town, I think you'll recognize every single

30
00:00:59,161 --> 00:01:00,184
place we stop.

31
00:01:00,534 --> 00:01:03,494
Our first stop is the corner drugstore.

32
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:05,112
Through the glass door,

33
00:01:05,112 --> 00:01:07,051
you can smell it before you see

34
00:01:07,051 --> 00:01:07,238
it.

35
00:01:07,418 --> 00:01:11,679
Medicine, perfume, coffee and fresh-made grilled cheese.

36
00:01:11,859 --> 00:01:12,675
On one side,

37
00:01:12,675 --> 00:01:15,598
the pharmacist works behind a high counter.

38
00:01:15,778 --> 00:01:17,065
On the other side,

39
00:01:17,065 --> 00:01:18,924
there's the soda fountain,

40
00:01:18,924 --> 00:01:21,213
with a long marble counter and a

41
00:01:21,213 --> 00:01:22,500
row of red stools.

42
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:25,511
The soda jerk wears a white paper

43
00:01:25,511 --> 00:01:25,833
hat.

44
00:01:26,013 --> 00:01:28,443
He makes a cherry Coke by pumping

45
00:01:28,443 --> 00:01:31,388
syrup into a glass and adding carbonated

46
00:01:31,388 --> 00:01:31,830
water.

47
00:01:32,010 --> 00:01:35,655
A chocolate malted costs about twenty-five cents.

48
00:01:35,835 --> 00:01:37,165
A banana split,

49
00:01:37,165 --> 00:01:38,761
maybe thirty-five.

50
00:01:38,941 --> 00:01:41,633
Teenagers crowd the booths after school,

51
00:01:41,633 --> 00:01:43,719
and old men drink coffee at the

52
00:01:43,719 --> 00:01:45,199
counter every morning.

53
00:01:45,549 --> 00:01:48,335
The pharmacist knows everyone in town by

54
00:01:48,335 --> 00:01:48,683
name.

55
00:01:48,863 --> 00:01:50,784
He knows who has a bad heart

56
00:01:50,784 --> 00:01:52,636
and whose baby has a cough.

57
00:01:52,816 --> 00:01:54,725
If you call late at night with

58
00:01:54,725 --> 00:01:55,553
a sick child,

59
00:01:55,553 --> 00:01:57,589
he might open the store just for

60
00:01:57,589 --> 00:01:57,844
you.

61
00:01:58,024 --> 00:02:00,439
There's a rack of comic books near

62
00:02:00,439 --> 00:02:02,925
the door, and a magazine stand with

63
00:02:02,925 --> 00:02:05,837
Life, Look and The Saturday Evening Post.

64
00:02:06,187 --> 00:02:08,311
Next door is the five-and-dime.

65
00:02:08,491 --> 00:02:11,134
Woolworth's, Kresge's, Ben Franklin,

66
00:02:11,134 --> 00:02:13,483
or a local store just like them.

67
00:02:13,663 --> 00:02:15,788
Wooden floors that creak when you walk.

68
00:02:15,968 --> 00:02:18,633
Long counters piled with everything you could

69
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:18,929
need.

70
00:02:19,109 --> 00:02:21,224
Thread, buttons, yarn,

71
00:02:21,224 --> 00:02:24,398
sewing patterns, kitchen gadgets,

72
00:02:24,398 --> 00:02:27,090
school supplies, cheap toys,

73
00:02:27,090 --> 00:02:29,302
goldfish and parakeets.

74
00:02:29,653 --> 00:02:31,490
Kids love the five-and-dime,

75
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:33,984
because it's the one store where their

76
00:02:33,984 --> 00:02:35,690
allowance goes a long way.

77
00:02:35,870 --> 00:02:37,876
A quarter can buy a bag of

78
00:02:37,876 --> 00:02:40,113
candy, a balsa wood airplane,

79
00:02:40,113 --> 00:02:41,965
or a little plastic toy.

80
00:02:42,145 --> 00:02:44,462
Many of those stores also have a

81
00:02:44,462 --> 00:02:47,359
lunch counter, where shoppers stop for a

82
00:02:47,359 --> 00:02:49,821
grilled cheese, a slice of pie and

83
00:02:49,821 --> 00:02:50,980
a cup of coffee.

84
00:02:51,331 --> 00:02:53,878
Across the street is the hardware store,

85
00:02:53,878 --> 00:02:55,661
and it's a world of its own.

86
00:02:55,841 --> 00:02:57,516
The smell of oil,

87
00:02:57,516 --> 00:02:59,881
rope and fresh-cut keys.

88
00:03:00,061 --> 00:03:02,784
Nails sold by the pound from wooden

89
00:03:02,784 --> 00:03:03,173
bins.

90
00:03:03,353 --> 00:03:04,641
Paint mixed by hand.

91
00:03:04,821 --> 00:03:07,422
Garden seeds in paper packets.

92
00:03:07,602 --> 00:03:09,860
Every tool a man could want,

93
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:11,956
hanging on pegboard walls.

94
00:03:12,306 --> 00:03:14,474
The owner can tell you exactly what

95
00:03:14,474 --> 00:03:16,334
you need, even if you can only

96
00:03:16,334 --> 00:03:17,077
describe it.

97
00:03:17,257 --> 00:03:18,978
You bring in the broken part,

98
00:03:18,978 --> 00:03:20,462
and he finds you a match.

99
00:03:20,642 --> 00:03:23,095
He'll explain how to fix a leaky

100
00:03:23,095 --> 00:03:25,854
faucet or sharpen a lawnmower blade.

101
00:03:26,034 --> 00:03:27,829
And if you're a little short on

102
00:03:27,829 --> 00:03:28,698
cash this week,

103
00:03:28,698 --> 00:03:30,377
he'll put it on your account.

104
00:03:30,727 --> 00:03:32,539
In the middle of the block is

105
00:03:32,539 --> 00:03:34,790
the biggest building on Main Street.

106
00:03:34,970 --> 00:03:36,305
The department store.

107
00:03:36,485 --> 00:03:37,687
In a bigger town,

108
00:03:37,687 --> 00:03:38,819
it might be a J.

109
00:03:38,999 --> 00:03:39,347
C.

110
00:03:39,527 --> 00:03:41,994
Penney, a Sears or a local family

111
00:03:41,994 --> 00:03:44,984
store with the founder's name painted on

112
00:03:44,984 --> 00:03:45,657
the side.

113
00:03:45,837 --> 00:03:48,812
Inside, there are glass display cases,

114
00:03:48,812 --> 00:03:51,709
high ceilings and an elevator with an

115
00:03:51,709 --> 00:03:54,370
operator who calls out each floor.

116
00:03:54,721 --> 00:03:56,926
Women shop for dresses,

117
00:03:56,926 --> 00:03:59,515
hats, gloves and stockings.

118
00:03:59,695 --> 00:04:01,034
Men buy suits,

119
00:04:01,034 --> 00:04:02,946
shirts and neckties.

120
00:04:03,126 --> 00:04:06,230
Children get measured for new school shoes,

121
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:08,901
and some stores have a machine called

122
00:04:08,901 --> 00:04:10,850
a shoe-fitting fluoroscope,

123
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:13,159
which uses X-rays so you can see

124
00:04:13,159 --> 00:04:15,758
your toes wiggling inside the shoes.

125
00:04:15,938 --> 00:04:17,854
It sounds incredible today,

126
00:04:17,854 --> 00:04:19,131
but kids loved it.

127
00:04:19,481 --> 00:04:20,309
When you pay,

128
00:04:20,309 --> 00:04:22,411
the clerk might put your money in

129
00:04:22,411 --> 00:04:24,831
a little container and send it zooming

130
00:04:24,831 --> 00:04:27,061
along a wire or through a pneumatic

131
00:04:27,061 --> 00:04:28,908
tube to the cashier upstairs.

132
00:04:29,088 --> 00:04:30,148
A moment later,

133
00:04:30,148 --> 00:04:32,339
your change comes zooming back.

134
00:04:32,519 --> 00:04:35,898
At Christmastime, the store windows are filled

135
00:04:35,898 --> 00:04:38,395
with displays, and Santa sits in a

136
00:04:38,395 --> 00:04:40,379
big chair on the toy floor.

137
00:04:40,729 --> 00:04:43,139
Down the street is the barbershop,

138
00:04:43,139 --> 00:04:44,698
with its spinning red,

139
00:04:44,698 --> 00:04:46,116
white and blue pole.

140
00:04:46,296 --> 00:04:49,093
Inside, there are two or three big

141
00:04:49,093 --> 00:04:52,385
leather chairs, a row of waiting chairs,

142
00:04:52,385 --> 00:04:54,771
and a stack of old magazines.

143
00:04:54,951 --> 00:04:57,273
A haircut costs around a dollar.

144
00:04:57,453 --> 00:04:59,356
The radio plays a ballgame,

145
00:04:59,356 --> 00:05:01,893
and the men argue about politics and

146
00:05:01,893 --> 00:05:02,457
fishing.

147
00:05:02,807 --> 00:05:04,922
Little boys get a crew cut or

148
00:05:04,922 --> 00:05:07,402
a flattop, sitting on a board laid

149
00:05:07,402 --> 00:05:09,517
across the arms of the chair.

150
00:05:09,697 --> 00:05:12,189
The barber finishes with a splash of

151
00:05:12,189 --> 00:05:14,403
tonic and a dusting of powder on

152
00:05:14,403 --> 00:05:15,026
the neck.

153
00:05:15,206 --> 00:05:16,724
And for the grown men,

154
00:05:16,724 --> 00:05:18,932
there's a hot towel shave with a

155
00:05:18,932 --> 00:05:21,692
straight razor, and maybe a shoeshine on

156
00:05:21,692 --> 00:05:22,520
the way out.

157
00:05:22,871 --> 00:05:24,885
A few doors away is the beauty

158
00:05:24,885 --> 00:05:27,102
shop, where the women of the town

159
00:05:27,102 --> 00:05:29,117
get their hair washed and set.

160
00:05:29,297 --> 00:05:31,579
They sit under big hooded dryers,

161
00:05:31,579 --> 00:05:34,485
reading magazines and catching up on every

162
00:05:34,485 --> 00:05:35,868
bit of news in town.

163
00:05:36,048 --> 00:05:38,701
Many people say the beauty shop was

164
00:05:38,701 --> 00:05:41,354
the real newspaper of a small town.

165
00:05:41,534 --> 00:05:42,409
If it happened,

166
00:05:42,409 --> 00:05:43,867
they knew about it there.

167
00:05:44,218 --> 00:05:46,383
On the corner stands the bank,

168
00:05:46,383 --> 00:05:47,465
built of stone,

169
00:05:47,465 --> 00:05:49,846
with tall columns and a big clock

170
00:05:49,846 --> 00:05:50,568
out front.

171
00:05:50,748 --> 00:05:52,888
Inside, the floors are marble,

172
00:05:52,888 --> 00:05:55,671
and the tellers sit behind brass cages.

173
00:05:55,851 --> 00:05:58,378
Children bring in their piggy banks to

174
00:05:58,378 --> 00:06:00,506
open a passbook savings account.

175
00:06:00,686 --> 00:06:03,465
And the bank president knows every farmer

176
00:06:03,465 --> 00:06:05,702
and business owner in the county.

177
00:06:06,052 --> 00:06:08,595
Across the street is the post office,

178
00:06:08,595 --> 00:06:11,346
with the American flag flying out front.

179
00:06:11,526 --> 00:06:14,491
Inside, there's a wall of little brass

180
00:06:14,491 --> 00:06:17,145
mailboxes with tiny glass windows.

181
00:06:17,325 --> 00:06:19,879
A stamp costs three cents.

182
00:06:20,059 --> 00:06:22,764
Wanted posters hang on the bulletin board.

183
00:06:22,944 --> 00:06:24,129
And the postmaster,

184
00:06:24,129 --> 00:06:26,373
who's held the job for twenty years,

185
00:06:26,373 --> 00:06:28,180
knows everyone's handwriting.

186
00:06:28,531 --> 00:06:30,603
And there's the newspaper office,

187
00:06:30,603 --> 00:06:32,989
with the presses rumbling in the back.

188
00:06:33,169 --> 00:06:35,444
The weekly paper reports everything.

189
00:06:35,624 --> 00:06:37,644
Who's visiting from out of town.

190
00:06:37,824 --> 00:06:39,845
Whose cow won at the county fair.

191
00:06:40,025 --> 00:06:41,904
Births, weddings, deaths,

192
00:06:41,904 --> 00:06:44,611
and the high school football scores.

193
00:06:44,791 --> 00:06:46,160
In a town like this,

194
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:48,420
getting your name in the paper is

195
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:49,516
a very big deal.

196
00:06:49,866 --> 00:06:51,503
By now, you're hungry.

197
00:06:51,683 --> 00:06:53,517
So let's stop at the diner.

198
00:06:53,697 --> 00:06:56,099
It might be a shiny steel railroad

199
00:06:56,099 --> 00:06:58,360
car diner, or a little café with

200
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,268
a counter and a few booths.

201
00:07:00,448 --> 00:07:02,730
The cook works the grill where everyone

202
00:07:02,730 --> 00:07:03,432
can see him.

203
00:07:03,612 --> 00:07:06,100
The waitress calls you honey and refills

204
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:07,780
your coffee before you ask.

205
00:07:08,130 --> 00:07:10,951
A hamburger costs about thirty cents.

206
00:07:11,131 --> 00:07:14,338
A plate of meatloaf with mashed potatoes

207
00:07:14,338 --> 00:07:17,946
and green beans is around seventy-five cents.

208
00:07:18,126 --> 00:07:19,612
And there's always pie.

209
00:07:19,792 --> 00:07:22,539
Apple, cherry, coconut cream,

210
00:07:22,539 --> 00:07:23,960
lemon meringue.

211
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:27,079
Every table has a little jukebox selector,

212
00:07:27,079 --> 00:07:29,388
where a nickel buys you one song.

213
00:07:29,738 --> 00:07:32,165
The diner is where the town really

214
00:07:32,165 --> 00:07:32,594
talks.

215
00:07:32,774 --> 00:07:34,693
Farmers come in early,

216
00:07:34,693 --> 00:07:36,001
before sunrise.

217
00:07:36,181 --> 00:07:38,259
Businessmen come for lunch.

218
00:07:38,439 --> 00:07:40,564
Teenagers come after the movie.

219
00:07:40,744 --> 00:07:43,670
Police officers stop by on their rounds.

220
00:07:43,850 --> 00:07:47,764
Everybody's welcome, and almost everybody stops in

221
00:07:47,764 --> 00:07:49,016
sooner or later.

222
00:07:49,366 --> 00:07:50,741
Halfway down the block,

223
00:07:50,741 --> 00:07:51,816
you can't miss it.

224
00:07:51,996 --> 00:07:53,136
The movie theater,

225
00:07:53,136 --> 00:07:55,290
with its big marquee full of light

226
00:07:55,290 --> 00:07:58,521
bulbs, and black letters announcing what's playing.

227
00:07:58,701 --> 00:08:00,460
In nineteen fifty-seven,

228
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:02,733
that might be The Bridge on the

229
00:08:02,733 --> 00:08:05,226
River Kwai, An Affair to Remember,

230
00:08:05,226 --> 00:08:07,792
or Elvis Presley in Jailhouse Rock.

231
00:08:08,141 --> 00:08:09,782
On Saturday afternoon,

232
00:08:09,782 --> 00:08:11,497
it's the kids' matinee.

233
00:08:11,677 --> 00:08:12,733
For a quarter,

234
00:08:12,733 --> 00:08:14,394
children get cartoons,

235
00:08:14,394 --> 00:08:16,884
a newsreel, a cowboy serial and a

236
00:08:16,884 --> 00:08:17,865
full feature.

237
00:08:18,045 --> 00:08:19,614
They throw popcorn,

238
00:08:19,614 --> 00:08:21,266
shout at the screen,

239
00:08:21,266 --> 00:08:23,165
and cheer for the hero.

240
00:08:23,345 --> 00:08:25,613
Parents drop them off at noon and

241
00:08:25,613 --> 00:08:27,057
pick them up at four,

242
00:08:27,057 --> 00:08:28,569
and everybody's happy.

243
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:31,219
Inside, the theater is grand,

244
00:08:31,219 --> 00:08:32,884
with velvet curtains,

245
00:08:32,884 --> 00:08:35,421
a balcony, and painted ceilings.

246
00:08:35,601 --> 00:08:37,824
An usher in a uniform shows you

247
00:08:37,824 --> 00:08:40,048
to your seat with a flashlight.

248
00:08:40,228 --> 00:08:42,062
Popcorn costs a dime.

249
00:08:42,242 --> 00:08:43,594
For many small towns,

250
00:08:43,594 --> 00:08:46,297
the theater is the most beautiful building

251
00:08:46,297 --> 00:08:48,485
in town, and the place where first

252
00:08:48,485 --> 00:08:50,416
dates and first kisses happen.

253
00:08:50,765 --> 00:08:52,530
As the sun goes down,

254
00:08:52,530 --> 00:08:54,295
Main Street fills up.

255
00:08:54,475 --> 00:08:56,963
Saturday night is the big night of

256
00:08:56,963 --> 00:08:57,621
the week.

257
00:08:57,801 --> 00:09:00,524
Farm families come into town to shop,

258
00:09:00,524 --> 00:09:02,143
visit and see a movie.

259
00:09:02,323 --> 00:09:04,262
Stores stay open late.

260
00:09:04,442 --> 00:09:07,063
People stroll up and down the sidewalk,

261
00:09:07,063 --> 00:09:09,953
stopping to talk with friends and neighbors

262
00:09:09,953 --> 00:09:11,768
they haven't seen all week.

263
00:09:12,119 --> 00:09:15,441
Teenagers cruise the street in their cars,

264
00:09:15,441 --> 00:09:16,865
around and around,

265
00:09:16,865 --> 00:09:18,051
radios playing.

266
00:09:18,231 --> 00:09:20,514
Old men sit on a bench outside

267
00:09:20,514 --> 00:09:21,960
the hardware store,

268
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:23,862
watching the world go by.

269
00:09:24,042 --> 00:09:26,551
Kids beg for one more ice cream

270
00:09:26,551 --> 00:09:26,956
cone.

271
00:09:27,136 --> 00:09:28,179
In many towns,

272
00:09:28,179 --> 00:09:30,785
there's a band concert in the park,

273
00:09:30,785 --> 00:09:33,242
or a dance at the American Legion

274
00:09:33,242 --> 00:09:33,615
hall.

275
00:09:33,964 --> 00:09:36,391
So what happened to that Main Street?

276
00:09:36,571 --> 00:09:39,498
In the years after nineteen fifty-seven,

277
00:09:39,498 --> 00:09:42,644
America changed in ways that slowly emptied

278
00:09:42,644 --> 00:09:42,864
it.

279
00:09:43,044 --> 00:09:44,902
The interstate highways,

280
00:09:44,902 --> 00:09:47,689
which started in nineteen fifty-six,

281
00:09:47,689 --> 00:09:50,167
made it easy to drive past small

282
00:09:50,167 --> 00:09:52,258
towns, or to drive out to a

283
00:09:52,258 --> 00:09:53,109
bigger one.

284
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:56,062
Shopping centers and malls grew up on

285
00:09:56,062 --> 00:09:57,258
the edge of town,

286
00:09:57,258 --> 00:10:00,213
with free parking and everything under one

287
00:10:00,213 --> 00:10:00,565
roof.

288
00:10:00,745 --> 00:10:04,054
The first fully enclosed mall in America,

289
00:10:04,054 --> 00:10:06,475
Southdale Center in Minnesota,

290
00:10:06,475 --> 00:10:08,977
had opened just a year earlier,

291
00:10:08,977 --> 00:10:10,753
in nineteen fifty-six.

292
00:10:10,933 --> 00:10:12,950
Over the next thirty years,

293
00:10:12,950 --> 00:10:15,043
thousands of malls followed.

294
00:10:15,393 --> 00:10:17,761
Then came the big discount stores.

295
00:10:17,941 --> 00:10:20,414
Kmart in nineteen sixty-two.

296
00:10:20,594 --> 00:10:22,428
Walmart, the same year.

297
00:10:22,608 --> 00:10:25,126
They offered lower prices than any small

298
00:10:25,126 --> 00:10:26,196
shop could match.

299
00:10:26,376 --> 00:10:27,262
One by one,

300
00:10:27,262 --> 00:10:30,000
the drugstore, the hardware store,

301
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,027
the five-and-dime and the department store closed.

302
00:10:34,207 --> 00:10:35,267
In many towns,

303
00:10:35,267 --> 00:10:37,690
the movie theater went dark too.

304
00:10:38,039 --> 00:10:39,786
By the nineteen eighties,

305
00:10:39,786 --> 00:10:42,161
many Main Streets were half empty.

306
00:10:42,341 --> 00:10:43,792
Boarded-up windows.

307
00:10:43,972 --> 00:10:45,342
For Lease signs.

308
00:10:45,522 --> 00:10:48,107
The bank building turned into a law

309
00:10:48,107 --> 00:10:50,027
office, or nothing at all.

310
00:10:50,207 --> 00:10:51,508
In nineteen eighty,

311
00:10:51,508 --> 00:10:55,070
the National Trust for Historic Preservation started

312
00:10:55,070 --> 00:10:56,714
the Main Street program,

313
00:10:56,714 --> 00:10:59,179
to try to bring those downtowns back

314
00:10:59,179 --> 00:10:59,727
to life.

315
00:11:00,078 --> 00:11:01,668
Some of them have come back.

316
00:11:01,848 --> 00:11:05,141
Today, many small towns have restored their

317
00:11:05,141 --> 00:11:06,442
old Main Streets,

318
00:11:06,442 --> 00:11:07,821
with coffee shops,

319
00:11:07,821 --> 00:11:10,730
bakeries and antique stores in the old

320
00:11:10,730 --> 00:11:11,496
buildings.

321
00:11:11,676 --> 00:11:13,278
But something is different.

322
00:11:13,458 --> 00:11:15,584
The pharmacist who knew your name,

323
00:11:15,584 --> 00:11:17,835
the barber who'd cut your hair since

324
00:11:17,835 --> 00:11:18,772
you were three,

325
00:11:18,772 --> 00:11:21,148
and the Saturday nights when the whole

326
00:11:21,148 --> 00:11:23,524
town came out are mostly memories now.

327
00:11:23,874 --> 00:11:25,963
What we lost wasn't just stores.

328
00:11:26,143 --> 00:11:27,537
It was a way of living.

329
00:11:27,717 --> 00:11:29,708
A place where you couldn't walk a

330
00:11:29,708 --> 00:11:32,001
block without saying hello to someone.

331
00:11:32,181 --> 00:11:34,468
Where business was done on a handshake.

332
00:11:34,648 --> 00:11:36,327
Where you were part of a town,

333
00:11:36,327 --> 00:11:37,446
not just a customer.

334
00:11:37,626 --> 00:11:38,598
For many people,

335
00:11:38,598 --> 00:11:40,482
that's what they miss the most.

336
00:11:40,832 --> 00:11:42,468
If you grew up on a Main

337
00:11:42,468 --> 00:11:43,627
Street like this,

338
00:11:43,627 --> 00:11:45,127
you carry it with you.

339
00:11:45,307 --> 00:11:46,805
The bell over the door.

340
00:11:46,985 --> 00:11:48,782
The squeak of the stool at the

341
00:11:48,782 --> 00:11:49,620
soda fountain.

342
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:51,391
The light of the marquee.

343
00:11:51,571 --> 00:11:53,765
The smell of the hardware store.

344
00:11:53,945 --> 00:11:55,113
It's still there,

345
00:11:55,113 --> 00:11:57,242
every time you close your eyes.

346
00:11:57,593 --> 00:11:59,427
So tell us in the comments.

347
00:11:59,607 --> 00:12:01,452
What was the name of your town,

348
00:12:01,452 --> 00:12:03,119
and what was the name of its

349
00:12:03,119 --> 00:12:03,833
Main Street?

350
00:12:04,013 --> 00:12:05,836
What store do you miss the most?

351
00:12:06,016 --> 00:12:07,432
We read every comment.

352
00:12:07,612 --> 00:12:09,694
Next, we're looking at what it was

353
00:12:09,694 --> 00:12:11,838
like to become a mother in nineteen

354
00:12:11,838 --> 00:12:12,512
fifty-five.

355
00:12:12,692 --> 00:12:14,539
Thank you for spending this time with

356
00:12:14,539 --> 00:12:14,688
me.
